<!--{ ty01 : rich }-->
# 5. Cell types

Every line has a **type**, which decides how it is edited and shown. The
icon in the left margin, before the line number, shows it. This lesson has
no checks: try each step and look at the result. About three minutes.
<!--{ ty02 : markdown }-->
## The types

| Type | Key | Shows |
|------|-----|-------|
| Rich text | **r** | Formatted text, edited as in Word (lesson 3). New lines are rich text. |
| Markdown | **m** | The same formatted text, written with Markdown characters, such as `**bold**`. |
| Text | **t** | The text without Markdown formatting: `**bold**` stays as it is written. |
| Formula | **=** | The result of one formula, edited without its brackets (lesson 4). |
| Table | **Shift+T** | A table, edited as a grid (lesson 6). |
| Data table | **d** | A table of values that other lines compute with (lesson 7). |
| Chart | **c** | A chart of values, such as two columns of a data table (lesson 7). |
<!--{ ty03 : rich }-->
## Change the type

Changing the type never changes the text of the line: only the way it is
edited and shown. Try it on the next line, which is written as
`This word is **bold**.`

1. Select it and press **t**: it becomes Text, and the stars show. Press
   **Escape** to leave it.
2. Press **m**: it becomes Markdown, and the word is bold again. **Escape**.
3. Press **r**: rich text, as you wrote in lesson 3. **Escape**.
<!--{ ty04 : markdown }-->
This word is **bold**.
<!--{ ty05 : rich }-->
A type's key changes the type and starts editing, so you can write at once.

## The type menu

To change several lines at once, or to see the types, select the lines and
press **Ctrl+/**, or click the icon in the left margin. A small menu opens,
with the current type checked and each type's key. Pick a type with the
mouse, or with **↑**, **↓** and **Enter**; **Escape** closes the menu.

## Only the types that fit

Because the text never changes, a line can only take a type that fits it: a
Formula cell needs one formula, a Table needs a table. In the menu the other
types are greyed out, with the reason. Try it: select the line above with
the bold word and press **=**. The line stays as it is, and a message says
why.

**Ctrl+Z** undoes a change of type.

More in the manual: [Cell types](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/cell-types.html).
<!--{ ty06 : rich }-->
